        "book-1": .extracted(
            title: "Chapter 1: The Harbor",
            byline: nil,
            siteName: nil,
            excerpt: "The harbor woke before the town did. Gulls circled the fishing boats while the first lamps came on along the quay, one window at a time, and the smell of coffee drifted down from the bakery on the corner.",
            textContent: "The harbor woke before the town did. Gulls circled the fishing boats while the first lamps came on along the quay, one window at a time, and the smell of coffee drifted down from the bakery on the corner.\nMara carried two baskets down the stone steps. She had made the walk every morning for eleven years, and she still counted the steps under her breath, the way her grandmother had taught her when she was small.\nAt the bottom, the harbor master was already arguing with the wind. He held his hat with one hand and a clipboard with the other, and he was losing on both counts, which seemed to make him argue all the harder.\nThe boats rocked against their ropes like restless horses. Somewhere a radio played a song that nobody would admit to knowing the words of, and somebody on a deck was humming along to it anyway, slightly off key.\nMara set the baskets down beside the ice house and looked out past the breakwater. The sea was grey and flat, the color of a spoon left out in the rain, and the horizon had quietly disappeared into the mist.\nThat is how the day began, the day the lighthouse went dark. Nobody noticed at first, because nobody ever looks at a light that is always on, any more than they listen for a clock that has always been ticking.",
            contentHTML: "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<style>\nbody{font:-apple-system-body;padding:16px;line-height:1.55;color:#111}\nh1{font:-apple-system-title1;margin-bottom:8px}\nimg{max-width:100%;height:auto}\n</style></head><body>\n<h1>Chapter 1: The Harbor</h1>\n\n<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><p>The harbor woke before the town did. Gulls circled the fishing boats while the first lamps came on along the quay, one window at a time, and the smell of coffee drifted down from the bakery on the corner.</p>\n<p>Mara carried two baskets down the stone steps. She had made the walk every morning for eleven years, and she still counted the steps under her breath, the way her grandmother had taught her when she was small.</p>\n<p>At the bottom, the harbor master was already arguing with the wind. He held his hat with one hand and a clipboard with the other, and he was losing on both counts, which seemed to make him argue all the harder.</p>\n<p>The boats rocked against their ropes like restless horses. Somewhere a radio played a song that nobody would admit to knowing the words of, and somebody on a deck was humming along to it anyway, slightly off key.</p>\n<p>Mara set the baskets down beside the ice house and looked out past the breakwater. The sea was grey and flat, the color of a spoon left out in the rain, and the horizon had quietly disappeared into the mist.</p>\n<p>That is how the day began, the day the lighthouse went dark. Nobody noticed at first, because nobody ever looks at a light that is always on, any more than they listen for a clock that has always been ticking.</p></div>\n</body></html>",
