        "book-2": .extracted(
            title: "Chapter 2: The Lighthouse",
            byline: nil,
            siteName: nil,
            excerpt: "The lighthouse stood on a rock a mile from shore, white and patient. Its keeper had retired years ago, and since then the light had looked after itself, turning every night without anyone needing to ask it.",
            textContent: "The lighthouse stood on a rock a mile from shore, white and patient. Its keeper had retired years ago, and since then the light had looked after itself, turning every night without anyone needing to ask it.\nBy noon the fishermen were talking about it in low voices. A light that fails in daylight is a small thing, they said to each other, until the evening comes and the boats are out and the fog rolls in.\nMara borrowed her uncle's rowing boat and a tin of tools from his shed. Her uncle did not ask her why she wanted them, which was his way of saying that he already knew, and that he thought she was right.\nThe crossing took the better part of an hour. The oars creaked, the water slapped at the bow, and the rock grew slowly larger in front of her until it filled the whole sky and she could hear the waves on it.\nInside, the spiral stairs smelled of salt and old paint. At the very top she found the great lens dusty but whole, and lying beside it a single burned out fuse, blackened at one end like a used match.\nShe replaced the fuse with one from the tin and flipped the switch. Nothing happened for a long moment, long enough for her to start doubting herself, and then the whole room filled with a warm and steady light.",
            contentHTML: "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<style>\nbody{font:-apple-system-body;padding:16px;line-height:1.55;color:#111}\nh1{font:-apple-system-title1;margin-bottom:8px}\nimg{max-width:100%;height:auto}\n</style></head><body>\n<h1>Chapter 2: The Lighthouse</h1>\n\n<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><p>The lighthouse stood on a rock a mile from shore, white and patient. Its keeper had retired years ago, and since then the light had looked after itself, turning every night without anyone needing to ask it.</p>\n<p>By noon the fishermen were talking about it in low voices. A light that fails in daylight is a small thing, they said to each other, until the evening comes and the boats are out and the fog rolls in.</p>\n<p>Mara borrowed her uncle's rowing boat and a tin of tools from his shed. Her uncle did not ask her why she wanted them, which was his way of saying that he already knew, and that he thought she was right.</p>\n<p>The crossing took the better part of an hour. The oars creaked, the water slapped at the bow, and the rock grew slowly larger in front of her until it filled the whole sky and she could hear the waves on it.</p>\n<p>Inside, the spiral stairs smelled of salt and old paint. At the very top she found the great lens dusty but whole, and lying beside it a single burned out fuse, blackened at one end like a used match.</p>\n<p>She replaced the fuse with one from the tin and flipped the switch. Nothing happened for a long moment, long enough for her to start doubting herself, and then the whole room filled with a warm and steady light.</p></div>\n</body></html>",
