        "book-3": .extracted(
            title: "Chapter 3: The Tide",
            byline: nil,
            siteName: nil,
            excerpt: "The tide had turned while she worked. When Mara came back down the stairs, the rowing boat was floating higher than she had left it, tugging at its line as if it were impatient to be going home again.",
            textContent: "The tide had turned while she worked. When Mara came back down the stairs, the rowing boat was floating higher than she had left it, tugging at its line as if it were impatient to be going home again.\nShe rowed back with the current behind her, which made the trip half as long as the crossing out. Behind her the beam swept across the water in slow and steady circles, the way it had for as long as she could remember.\nOn the quay a small crowd had gathered to wait for her. Nobody cheered, because that is not what people do in a harbor town, but several of them nodded at once, and one of them helped her tie up the boat.\nThe harbor master wrote something on his clipboard and underlined it twice. Later she learned that it said only the date and the word fixed, which from him was about as close to a speech as anyone ever got.\nThat night the boats went out as usual, and came home again guided by a light that nobody noticed, because nobody ever looks at a light that is always on, until the one evening when it is not.\nAnd that is the end of the demo book. If you heard this last line, playback carried on through every chapter of the book and then stopped by itself at the end, which is exactly what it is supposed to do.",
            contentHTML: "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<style>\nbody{font:-apple-system-body;padding:16px;line-height:1.55;color:#111}\nh1{font:-apple-system-title1;margin-bottom:8px}\nimg{max-width:100%;height:auto}\n</style></head><body>\n<h1>Chapter 3: The Tide</h1>\n\n<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><p>The tide had turned while she worked. When Mara came back down the stairs, the rowing boat was floating higher than she had left it, tugging at its line as if it were impatient to be going home again.</p>\n<p>She rowed back with the current behind her, which made the trip half as long as the crossing out. Behind her the beam swept across the water in slow and steady circles, the way it had for as long as she could remember.</p>\n<p>On the quay a small crowd had gathered to wait for her. Nobody cheered, because that is not what people do in a harbor town, but several of them nodded at once, and one of them helped her tie up the boat.</p>\n<p>The harbor master wrote something on his clipboard and underlined it twice. Later she learned that it said only the date and the word fixed, which from him was about as close to a speech as anyone ever got.</p>\n<p>That night the boats went out as usual, and came home again guided by a light that nobody noticed, because nobody ever looks at a light that is always on, until the one evening when it is not.</p>\n<p>And that is the end of the demo book. If you heard this last line, playback carried on through every chapter of the book and then stopped by itself at the end, which is exactly what it is supposed to do.</p></div>\n</body></html>",
